    System.out.println("str1 and str2 have the same value");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 have different values");
}
```

In this example, both `str1` and `str2` contain the same sequence of characters, but they are different objects in memory. Therefore, the output of the program will be:

```
str1 and str2 are different objects
str1 and str2 have the same value
```

To summarize:

- Use the `==` operator to compare reference types for equality (i.e., to check if two variables refer to the same object).
- Use the `.equals()` method to compare the values of objects for equality (i.e., to check if two objects contain the same data).

In general, when comparing strings in Java, you should always use the `.equals()` method instead of the `==` operator. This will ensure that you are comparing the actual values of the strings, not just their memory references.

